WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.355
Good morning, John. I have some good news.

00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:04.875
Some bad news, but some good news. There's this number

00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:07.965
that people use as part of, uh, our understanding of public health

00:00:07.965 --> 00:00:10.105
in the world. Kind of a surrogate for the overall health

00:00:10.105 --> 00:00:13.205
of a population, though not a great one. It's called

00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:16.255
life expectancy. Very simply, it is the average age at which

00:00:16.255 --> 00:00:19.635
a person in our country will die.  That number has gone up a lot

00:00:19.695 --> 00:00:22.475
in the last 200 years for people but

00:00:22.475 --> 00:00:25.745
interestingly, it's also gone up for you...

00:00:25.805 --> 00:00:28.875
person watching.  And it goes up for you

00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:30.925
every day. And probably not

00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:33.815
for the reason you're thinking.  So the life expectancy of the average dude in the

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.195
U.S. is 76. But for me,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.180
it's 78. That's nice!  I got an extra couple of years

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.800
on the average dude.  Look at me! How did I do that?

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.600
Well, here's a statistic that might sound a little more surprising at first but will

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.585
help you figure out why. The average person will die

00:00:49.585 --> 00:00:52.420
at 76, but the average 76 year old

00:00:52.425 --> 00:00:55.455
will die at 86.  And the average 86

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.460
year old ,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.320
they will die at 91. So I was hanging out with Thomas Franker earlier

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.420
today of college info geek, he's 26 years old.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.225
His life expectancy is 77 years old.

00:01:05.225 --> 00:01:08.265
Mine is 78. What is up with that is that he has to live

00:01:08.265 --> 00:01:10.985
for like 11 more years

00:01:10.985 --> 00:01:14.115
before he gets to my age. Very sadly,  some

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:17.225
people will die, not very many, but some,  between the

00:01:17.225 --> 00:01:23.520
ages of 26 and 37. I, on the other hand,  because I am 37, will never have died

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.900
between the ages of 26 and 37. I just can't do it.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.700
So every hour you live, your life expectancy goes up.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.680
The number of hours you have left to live goes down

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.620
but not at one hour per hour.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.620
Every hour that passes – this makes my brain hurt –

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:46.500
you are closer to death, but you are less than one hour closer to death.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.000
And that's a small comfort.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.000
I had this dumb idea, when I was younger,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.160
that since the life expectancy of a person in America is 78,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.820
that once you got to 78 you could just die at any time.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.820
Like a stiff wind blows and you're just like "I'm out"

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.680
But it is not until

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.515
You are 114 that you have

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.820
A better than average chance of dying in the next

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.875
365 days. WHAT?

00:02:07.875 --> 00:02:10.925
Now to be fair, there are many years before that when you have

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:13.765
A better than average chance of dying in the next thousand days

00:02:13.765 --> 00:02:15.255
Which is why most people don't get

00:02:15.255 --> 00:02:18.035
To 114. But by and large, statistically

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.680
Everyone can expect to have at least a year

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.895
When you turn 114 though, you have a more than 50% chance of

00:02:23.895 --> 00:02:27.155
Hitting 115, that's the statistics

00:02:27.155 --> 00:02:30.045
Another thing worth noting is that life expectancy takes a snapshot of

00:02:30.045 --> 00:02:32.425
This very moment with the death rates for

00:02:32.425 --> 00:02:35.505
Every person at every age. It is actually not a measure of when

00:02:35.505 --> 00:02:38.455
You will die, because that death rate

00:02:38.455 --> 00:02:41.485
Will change over time, and everyone knows that. And mostly

00:02:41.485 --> 00:02:44.520
We hope, barring some kind of catastrophic solar flare induced apocalypse

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.840
Death rates will continue to go down. Like the life expectancy for a 25 year old

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.740
In 1960 was about 70 years old, but it turned out

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.140
That that 25 year old, or the population of 25 year olds

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.900
Ended up dying significantly after the age of 70, so yeah, that thing

00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:01.175
Which doesn't actually have to do with life expectancy the statistic

00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:02.555
Makes your life longer

00:03:02.865 --> 00:03:05.955
And nobody has to die, so that John, is a plus

